WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.081
Picture a recipe step that goes wrong.

2
00:00:05.081 --> 00:00:06.271
The custard splits.

3
00:00:06.271 --> 00:00:11.910
A calm cook hands back a note: the custard split, let's serve the cake with cream instead.

4
00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:17.361
An upset cook throws the pan across the room, and someone further away has to catch it.

5
00:00:17.567 --> 00:00:22.087
In code, a returned value is the note, and an exception is the thrown pan.

6
00:00:22.087 --> 00:00:26.668
And earlier in this lesson, we built a kitchen line where the notes travel.

7
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:28.633
Here it is again.

8
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:31.550
A good batter walks down the line, step by step.

9
00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:40.847
When a step fails, the batter leaves through a side door with a note pinned to it, and the note walks straight to the end, past every step still waiting.

10
00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:44.780
But here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

11
00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:50.928
When my code checks several things, should it stop at the first problem, or tell me about all of them?

12
00:00:50.928 --> 00:00:52.133
Does it even matter?

13
00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:55.107
Let's start with a line of steps.

14
00:00:55.107 --> 00:01:02.278
The app orders groceries: reserve the ingredient at the shop, check that the delivery arrives in time, then book it.

15
00:01:02.278 --> 00:01:04.195
Each step needs the one before.

16
00:01:04.195 --> 00:01:06.853
You can't book a basket you never reserved.

17
00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:11.706
With our hand-written result type, the steps chain with andThen.

18
00:01:11.706 --> 00:01:12.259
It works.

19
00:01:12.259 --> 00:01:17.429
But each step nests inside the one before, and the happy path hides in the brackets.

20
00:01:17.633 --> 00:01:23.762
Arrow, a functional programming library for Kotlin, has the same kind of result type, called Either.

21
00:01:23.762 --> 00:01:28.725
An Either holds one of two things: a Left with an error, or a Right with a value.

22
00:01:28.725 --> 00:01:32.096
And its either block lets the steps read top to bottom.

23
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.358
Inside the block, bind unwraps a Right.

24
00:01:35.358 --> 00:01:39.952
On a Left, it stops the block right there, and that Left becomes the result.

25
00:01:39.952 --> 00:01:43.761
ensure checks a condition, and stops the block when it's false.

26
00:01:43.761 --> 00:01:46.360
That's the side door, built into the block.

27
00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:49.266
This style is called fail fast.

28
00:01:49.266 --> 00:01:52.492
The first failure wins, and nothing after it runs.

29
00:01:52.492 --> 00:01:55.201
For a line of steps, that's exactly right.

30
00:01:56.133 --> 00:01:58.096
Now a different job: a form.

31
00:01:58.096 --> 00:02:04.825
A cook types an amount of minus 5, with the unit oz, because the scale was left on ounces again.

32
00:02:04.825 --> 00:02:05.736
Two problems.

33
00:02:05.736 --> 00:02:11.904
The amount must be more than zero, and the app only knows grams, millilitres and pieces.

34
00:02:12.833 --> 00:02:16.977
Here are the two checks, and a fail fast block that uses both.

35
00:02:16.977 --> 00:02:20.185
What does failFast give back for minus 5 and oz?

36
00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:26.195
Either.Left, InvalidAmount, minus 5.

37
00:02:26.195 --> 00:02:27.121
That's all.

38
00:02:27.121 --> 00:02:29.224
The unit check never ran.

39
00:02:30.133 --> 00:02:34.974
So the cook fixes the amount, saves again, and only now hears about the ounces.

40
00:02:34.974 --> 00:02:41.041
It's like a taster who says needs salt, waits for you to salt it, and then mentions that it's cold.

41
00:02:41.041 --> 00:02:46.495
Jokes aside: when the checks don't need each other, one complaint per try is a poor deal.

42
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.606
What we want is a kitchen inspector with a clipboard.

43
00:02:50.606 --> 00:02:56.594
She walks the whole form, writes down every problem she finds, and hands you the full list at once.

44
00:02:56.594 --> 00:03:00.284
And only if the clipboard stays empty does the dish get made.

45
00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.029
Let's map it.

46
00:03:02.029 --> 00:03:04.324
Each check is one stop on her round.

47
00:03:04.324 --> 00:03:06.619
The clipboard is the list of errors.

48
00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:10.891
And the dish, here an Amount, is made only when every check passed.

49
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.592
Arrow's inspector is called zipOrAccumulate.

50
00:03:14.592 --> 00:03:22.523
It runs checks that don't depend on each other, collects every failure, and calls its last lambda only if all of them passed.

51
00:03:22.733 --> 00:03:25.045
Same input, minus 5 and oz.

52
00:03:25.045 --> 00:03:26.757
What comes back now?

53
00:03:29.733 --> 00:03:35.549
Either.Left, with a list: InvalidAmount, minus 5, and UnsupportedUnit, oz.

54
00:03:35.549 --> 00:03:37.278
Both problems at once.

55
00:03:37.278 --> 00:03:42.937
And with a good amount and unit, 250 g, it's a Right holding the Amount.

56
00:03:43.167 --> 00:03:44.976
Look at the type of that Left.

57
00:03:44.976 --> 00:03:48.835
It's a NonEmptyList, a list that always holds at least one item.

58
00:03:48.835 --> 00:03:52.936
If there's a Left, there's at least one error, and the type says so.

59
00:03:53.867 --> 00:03:55.144
Java has no Either.

60
00:03:55.144 --> 00:04:02.605
The lesson's Java version does the same job by hand: a list of errors, one if per rule, and a check at the end.

61
00:04:03.533 --> 00:04:06.194
And here's where the clipboard picture breaks.

62
00:04:06.194 --> 00:04:09.029
The inspector can only look at things that exist.

63
00:04:09.029 --> 00:04:12.731
She can't check the booking of a basket that was never reserved.

64
00:04:12.731 --> 00:04:16.317
When a check needs the one before, there's nothing to collect.

65
00:04:16.317 --> 00:04:18.457
That's a line, and a line fails fast.

66
00:04:19.367 --> 00:04:20.483
So here's the rule.

67
00:04:20.483 --> 00:04:24.007
Stop at the first error when each step needs the one before.

68
00:04:24.007 --> 00:04:27.707
Collect every error when the checks don't depend on each other.

69
00:04:28.633 --> 00:04:31.025
One more tool, for code that throws.

70
00:04:31.025 --> 00:04:34.547
Either.catch turns an ordinary exception into a Left.

71
00:04:34.547 --> 00:04:40.195
Parse 2OO, typed with two letter Os, and you get a Left with a NumberFormatException.

72
00:04:40.195 --> 00:04:45.045
But it lets a CancellationException through, the one that carries a stop.

73
00:04:45.045 --> 00:04:48.101
That makes it the safer cousin of runCatching.

74
00:04:49.033 --> 00:04:50.543
So the picker is short.

75
00:04:50.543 --> 00:04:53.431
A nullable type when nothing needs a reason.

76
00:04:53.431 --> 00:04:56.647
A sealed type for a few outcomes of one function.

77
00:04:56.647 --> 00:05:00.454
Either when many steps chain, or errors must be collected.

78
00:05:00.454 --> 00:05:02.357
And exceptions stay for bugs.

79
00:05:03.267 --> 00:05:06.585
So, stop at the first problem, or report them all?

80
00:05:06.585 --> 00:05:11.031
It comes down to one question: does each check need the one before?

81
00:05:11.031 --> 00:05:12.225
A line fails fast.

82
00:05:12.225 --> 00:05:13.619
A clipboard collects.

83
00:05:14.333 --> 00:05:15.141
Quick check.

84
00:05:15.141 --> 00:05:20.724
A recipe form checks a title and a cooking time, and neither check needs the other.

85
00:05:20.724 --> 00:05:22.204
Fail fast, or collect?

86
00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:25.735
Collect.

87
00:05:25.735 --> 00:05:30.486
The checks are independent, so the cook should hear about both at once.

88
00:05:30.486 --> 00:05:32.025
That's zipOrAccumulate.
